I've finished the edit stage for Dan Ives and written the three outputs. Type 7 at medium confidence stands, and all three files pass the contract's checks: no draft blockers and a valid evidence record. Nothing was published, and none of the input files were touched.

**The main change.** All six reviewers pointed at the same flaw: the Seven-over-Three argument said he "avoided the critics" and never repaired his image. The record shows otherwise:
- He went on CNBC's Fast Money the Monday after the Barron's cover story.
- He defended his outside roles on a friend's podcast.
- He started adding disclosures to his X posts.
- He quit the crypto company's board six weeks later.
- In July his publicist set up an interview with the same reporters he had blocked.

The article now says those moves fit a Three at least as well as a Seven. The Seven read rests on two narrower things: how he explains his choices (freedom, "constricting", "the treadmill", refusing to live by regret) and his habit of answering a cost with an upside. The article says plainly that this is mostly self-report from friendly interviews, which is why confidence stays medium. It also names what would settle it: if his new firm narrows into one focused build, the Three reading gains.

